# Backend configuration.
#
# Nothing in the app reads this file. It is a checklist, and a thing to hand to
# whatever does set the environment — systemd's EnvironmentFile=, a container's
# env_file, or `set -a; . ./.env; set +a` in a shell. Copy it to .env (which is
# gitignored) and fill it in there.
#
# Every one of these has a development default, so the server starts with none
# of them set. Two of those defaults are insecure and the server says so
# loudly at boot — if you see a [config] WARNING in a deployed log, something
# here is missing.

# Signs and verifies auth tokens. MUST be set in production: the fallback is
# published in this repository, so anyone could mint a token for any account.
# Any long random string will do, for example:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=

# Postgres connection. The development default uses the password that is
# already public in this repository, so it must not survive a deployment.
#   DATABASE_DSN='host=localhost user=kutubhane password=... dbname=school_library port=5432 sslmode=disable'
# Add sslmode=require if Postgres is not on the same machine.
#
# QUOTE IT. Anything that reads this file with `set -a; . ./.env` is the shell
# parsing it, and this value has spaces in: unquoted, only `host=localhost`
# reaches the app and `user`, `password` and `dbname` become three separate
# variables nothing looks at. Single quotes rather than double, so a `$` in a
# password stays a `$`.
DATABASE_DSN=

# Which browser origins may call this API, comma-separated, no trailing slash.
# Needed only when the UI is served from somewhere else; if STATIC_DIR below
# serves it from this same process, nothing is cross-origin and this can stay
# empty. "*" is not accepted — the API is called with credentials.
#   CORS_ORIGINS=https://demo.example.az
CORS_ORIGINS=

# A directory of built frontend files (library-frontend/dist) for this server
# to hand out alongside the API: one process, one origin, no CORS. Leave empty
# if a web server or CDN serves the UI instead.
#   STATIC_DIR=/srv/kutubhane/dist
STATIC_DIR=

# Port to listen on. Defaults to 8000.
#
# Leave this EMPTY on cPanel's Application Manager. Passenger picks a port,
# puts it here itself, and listens for the app on it; setting your own means
# the app listens where Passenger is not looking and every request times out.
PORT=

# Which interface to listen on. Empty is all of them — right on a machine that
# is yours, and convenient in development. On shared hosting it is not: every
# other account on the box can reach a port bound that widely, and the only
# thing that should connect is the proxy in front.
#   BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
BIND_ADDRESS=

# Set to true ONLY when a reverse proxy you control (the Caddy or nginx in
# DEPLOY.md) sits in front and sets X-Forwarded-For.
#
# The login rate limiter keys on the caller's address. Behind a proxy without
# this, every request appears to come from 127.0.0.1 and the whole internet
# shares one bucket. With this but *without* a proxy, the address is whatever
# the client typed, and an attacker has an unlimited supply of them. Neither
# default is safe for the other case, so it is a decision.
TRUST_PROXY=

# GORM_LOG=info logs every SQL statement (useful while developing, slow under
# load); silent logs none. The default logs warnings only.
GORM_LOG=
